DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ssaa reads the raw accounting records written by the kernel's
     aacccctt() mechanism from _f_i_l_e (default /uussrr/aaddmm/aacccctt) and
     prints, for each distinct command name, the number of times
     it was called, its total CPU time (user plus system), and
     its total real time.  Output is sorted by decreasing CPU
     time unless -bb or -nn is given.  Before scanning the raw
     file, ssaa reads the summary files /uussrr/aaddmm/ssaavvaacccctt and
     /uussrr/aaddmm/uussrraacccctt so that totals accumulate across runs.

     If -uu is given, ssaa instead prints, for every record in _f_i_l_e,
     the user name and command name directly, and does no sum-
     marizing at all.

     Options:

     -aa   Commands that were called only once, or whose name con-
          tains a non-printable character, are folded into the
          single category ***ootthheerr.

     -bb   Sort by average CPU time per call, instead of by total
          CPU time.

     -cc   Also print each command's CPU time as a percentage of
          the total CPU time used by all commands.

     -jj   Print average time per call rather than totals for each
          command.

     -ll   Print user time and system time in separate columns,
          instead of their sum.

     -mm   Accumulate and print statistics per user rather than
          per command.

     -nn   Sort by number of calls, instead of by CPU time.

     -rr   Reverse the sort order.

     -ss   After the raw file has been read and summarized, merge
          the results back into /uussrr/aaddmm/ssaavvaacccctt and /uussrr/aaddmm/uuss-
          rraacccctt, and truncate _f_i_l_e.

     -tt   Also print each command's CPU time as a percentage of
          its real time.

     -uu   Print the user name and command name directly from the
          raw accounting file, one line per record, rather than a
          summary.


